baryon-number.conceptual_definition:
The Baryon Number (
B
) is a conserved additive quantum number that characterizes particles made of quarks (baryons). It is defined as the integral of the time component of the baryon current density over all of space. This integral quantifies the total number of baryons minus the total number of antibaryons within a given region of spacetime.

baryon-number.symmetry_origin:
The conservation of the baryon number arises from a global U(1) symmetry of the Lagrangian under which quark fields change by a phase factor. According to Noether's theorem, every continuous symmetry of a physical system corresponds to a conserved current and a conserved charge. In this case, the symmetry associated with the baryon number leads to a conserved baryon current (
J_B^
u
) and consequently, the conserved baryon number (
B
) which is the time-integrated spatial density of this current.
